Now I understand the vulnerability. `isNumeric` does `chartype_table[static_cast<size_t>(in)]`. If `in` is a `char` with a negative value (e.g., a byte >= 0x80), then `static_cast<size_t>(in)` will be a huge value (sign-extended), causing an out-of-bounds read of `chartype_table`.

The crash state is:
- `bool ODDLParser::isNumeric`
- `ODDLParser::OpenDDLParser::parseIdentifier`
- `ODDLParser::OpenDDLParser::parseName`

So the path is: `parseName` -> `parseIdentifier` -> `isNumeric(*in)` where `*in` is a byte >= 0x80.

Let me look at `parseName` and `parseIdentifier` more carefully. In `parseName`:
```cpp
char *OpenDDLParser::parseName(char *in, char *end, Name **name) {
    *name = nullptr;
    if (nullptr == in || in == end) {
        return in;
    }
    // ignore blanks
    in = lookForNextToken(in, end);
    if (*in != '$' && *in != '%') {
        return in;
    }
    ...
    in++;
    Name *currentName(nullptr);
    Text *id(nullptr);
    in = parseIdentifier(in, end, &id);
    ...
}
```

In `parseIdentifier`:
```cpp
char *OpenDDLParser::parseIdentifier(char *in, char *end, Text **id) {
    *id = nullptr;
    if (nullptr == in || in == end) {
        return in;
    }
    // ignore blanks
    in = lookForNextToken(in, end);
    if (in == end) {
        return in;
    }
    // staring with a number is forbidden
    if (isNumeric<const char>(*in)) {   // <-- OOB here if *in is a byte >= 0x80
        return in;
    }
    ...
}
```

So we need a byte >= 0x80 at the position where `parseIdentifier` checks `isNumeric(*in)`. This happens after `parseName` sees `$` or `%` and increments past it.

Let me look at `lookForNextToken` to understand the flow.

